dee_shim modified by beq-encoder: give a quiet object a weight so that it gets its own cluster

Upstream: https://github.com/truehdd/dee-shim/issues/4

Applies to https://github.com/truehdd/dee-shim at 294c9d1 (v0.1.0). Under --oamd-as-is the
shim clears the silence flag of an element whose block is not all zero, but DEE's candidate
filter admits an element by its weight column, which DEE's preprocessor leaves at zero for a
block its silence detector (about 1.5 LSB) calls silent. Such an element gets no cluster and
is panned into its neighbours' clusters: an onset from digital silence comes back 1 LSB off,
and an object held under the threshold rides in another object's cluster. This gives a live
element with an all-zero column a weight of 0.25, at or above the lowered threshold and under
the stock 0.5, so it is a candidate exactly when the frame fits.

Measured with DEE 6.7.0 (Linux port) and truehdd 0.6.2: Klaus (Cavern decode of DD+ Atmos,
LFE + 15 objects, 6 min) 260 samples off by 1 LSB before, 0 of 277,266,432 after; Edge of
Tomorrow (TrueHD Atmos, LFE + 11 objects, 6 min) 0 before and after; 42 synthetic onset,
fade and sub-threshold cases 0 after.

